edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148260 #edb/ref 1061 151917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d4fc228-ca4d-50f3-8d5b-ccf710a05837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148261 #edb/ref 1061 151921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148261 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b18f6f42-9f33-5dcc-b9b0-bc6413ee5bb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148261 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1061 632742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9c7592c-10c0-5cc3-b1ff-b5b67dfe08ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1061 632743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5df02fb6-3576-5996-8b1e-64978fa7b5d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1061 632746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6835341-5e27-57e3-ae67-a97b6df1ba75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1061 632749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ce04142-5c86-5b4c-a76f-55af88901484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1061 632750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1061 632753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1061 632754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1065 14935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1065 14937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1064 "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1065 2078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148791 #edb/ref 1064 "Any multiplication of fractions can also be interpreted as multiplication and division of whole numbers.\n\nFor example, consider\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{\\color{blue}3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nWriting the fractions as division, our expression becomes\n$(14\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 3}})\\times (6\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})$.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the two divisions. Swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 7}}$, we get the following equivalent expression:\n$(14\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}) \\times (6\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 3}})$\n\nIf we interpret the divisions as fractions again, then we have another equivalent expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{\\color{red}7} \\times \\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNotice that this amounts to *swapping the denominators* in the original expression." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148791 #edb/ref 1065 8589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148791 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c473aa48-4570-5eb6-9482-3e3e9c088ea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148791 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148792 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving a fraction multiplication problem, it's often convenient to simplify the problem *before* carrying out the multiplication.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}12$) have a common\nfactor of $3$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}12} \\times \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the first fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 1}{4 \\times 8} = \\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148792 #edb/ref 1065 8591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148792 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c36b33ff-a822-5308-95a8-990652f0aab8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148792 #edb/ref 1067 "Evaluating Products of Fractions Using a Strategy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148798 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use our knowledge of decimal multiplication to evaluate decimal exponents.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $(0.4)^2$.\n\nFirst, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.4)^{2} &= 0.4 \\cdot 0.4_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute this product, we first ignore any decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We also ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}2$ original factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $0.4$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(0.4)^{\\color{blue}2}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\color{red}2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $16$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(0.4)^2 = 0.16\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148798 #edb/ref 1065 8957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148798 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d99a8d89-2de3-5681-a3e1-6a6aa68ab428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148798 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Numbers as Natural" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Numbers as Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Numbers as Rational" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Rational Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1045 "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1061 172680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1061 172681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1061 172684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1061 172685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1061 172688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1061 172689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1061 172692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1061 172693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1064 "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1065 9069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1064 "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1065 9070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1067 "The Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1065 9071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1065 9072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1067 "Repeating Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157306 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 2, 5, or 10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157306 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157306 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "41a2fa93-ff99-507f-98ca-3db7347f843f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157307 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a25bce44-98ca-54c6-a59f-b7508880d5ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157318 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157318 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157318 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157318 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "50cd6e99-6275-5a6a-9c91-d316392ec8ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1045 "Divisibility of Integers by 2, 5, 10, 4, and 8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157469 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "edd51738-a7e7-5119-a156-2948a25e0822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158036 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158036 #edb/ref 1061 172421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc976df9-d5c5-512d-a6c4-5b7fa78feb28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158153 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158153 #edb/ref 1061 27826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158153 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d87beb1-3c40-501d-9b47-96a06e457197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158153 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158154 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158154 #edb/ref 1061 27827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158154 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e56c82e-779e-500a-86ec-09a1f92663f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158154 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158165 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158165 #edb/ref 1061 27848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158165 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "545a5913-05ca-53cd-ba7f-28108c16e428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158165 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158166 #edb/ref 1061 27849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158166 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9dbfbbc-e3ad-5ff2-b1bf-89b8dc9c4147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158166 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158173 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158173 #edb/ref 1061 28079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158173 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6db52270-24c1-5608-a3a7-fd26378cec20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158173 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158490 #edb/ref 1064 "Dividing very large numbers can take some effort. Thankfully, there are some rules that allow us to determine whether one number is divisible by another *without* having to carry out the division!\n\nA number is divisible by $2$ if the *last digit* is an even number. In other words, if the last digit is ${\\color{blue}{0}},{\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{blue}{4}},{\\color{blue}{6}}$, or ${\\color{blue}{8}}$, then the number is divisible by $2$.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are divisible by $2$ because their last digit is even.\n$51{\\color{blue}{0}}, \\qquad 785{\\color{blue}{2}}, \\qquad 232,65{\\color{blue}{4}}, \\qquad 197,478,24{\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad 578,197,478,24{\\color{blue}{8}}$\n\nOn the other hand, the following numbers are *not* divisible by $2$ because their last digit is *not* even.\n$35{\\color{red}{1}}, \\qquad 547{\\color{red}{3}}, \\qquad 58,68{\\color{red}{5}}, \\qquad 239,63{\\color{red}{7}}, \\qquad 100,000,000,00{\\color{red}{9}}$\n\nWe also have some easy divisibility rules for $5$ and $10$. Again, they depend only on the last digit:\n\n- A number is divisible by $5$ if the *last digit* is $5$ or $0$.\n- A number is divisible by $10$ if the *last digit* is $0$.\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158490 #edb/ref 1065 4524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158490 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f79c4bec-8582-50b0-b80c-7a153bd0fbf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158490 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158496 #edb/ref 1064 "A number is divisible by $8$ if the last three digits form a number that is divisible by $8$.\n\nFor example, consider the number $845\\,128\\,016$. The last three digits form the number $016 = 16$, which is divisible by $8$. Therefore, the number $845\\,128\\,016$ is divisible by $8$.\n\nOkay, that was easy. But what about the number $745\\,254\\,786\\,768$? Is this divisible by $8$?\n\nThe last three digits form the number $768$. Dividing $768$ by $8$ requires a bit more work. However, there is an easy rule to check whether a $3$-digit number is divisible by $8$. That is, we add four times the hundreds digit to twice the tens digit and the ones digit, and then check if the result is divisible by $8$. In our case, we have\n\n$$\n4(7) + 2(6) + 8 = 28+12+8 = 48\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $8$. Therefore, $745\\,254\\,786\\,768$ is divisible by $8$.\n\nFinally, note that if a number is not divisible by $2$ *and* $4$, then it cannot be divisible by $8$. Sometimes, it's easiest to check those first." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158496 #edb/ref 1065 4570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b5e0fe81-8ae3-5806-b8cf-3039fdc050a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158496 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158604 #edb/ref 1064 "A number is divisible by $4$ if the *last two digits* are divisible by $4$.\n\nLet's determine whether the following number is divisible by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n124, 424, 6{\\color{blue}{24}}\n$$\n\nThe number formed by the last two digits is ${\\color{blue}{24}}$, and ${\\color{blue}{24}}$ is divisible by $4$. Therefore, $124, 424, 624$ is divisible by $4$.\n\nNow, consider the following number:\n\n$$\n251, 8{\\color{red}{18}}\n$$\n\nThe number formed by the last two digits is ${\\color{red}{18}}$, and ${\\color{red}{18}}$ is *not* divisible by $4$. Therefore, $251, 818$ is *not* divisible by $4$.\n\nWe sometimes need to use long division to determine whether the last two digits are divisible by $4$.\n\nFor example, the number\n$1,7{\\color{blue}{84}}$\nis divisible by $4$ because ${\\color{blue}{84}}$ is divisible by $4$. We can check this using long division.\n\n| $2$$1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nFinally, note that if a number is divisible by $4$, then it is divisible by $2$. Consequently, any number whose last digit is odd is *not* divisible by $4$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158604 #edb/ref 1065 9438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158604 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91876d6f-1b93-5d6d-adf9-a0af7e58aca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158604 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178391 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4c3846fd-4837-5e23-8b1b-cfd0f2851058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178395 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x}{1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178395 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/r