127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180127 #edb/ref 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"The quotient of a number and one fewer than that number.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "707758b3-8efc-56c2-97b0-ced821d3d5f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180127 #edb/ref 1074 "If we denote our number by $x$, then \"one fewer than that number\" can be written as\n\n$$\nx-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the whole phrase can be written as \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180632 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180632 #edb/ref 1061 77833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180632 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "935e508e-1137-5092-83aa-7b9d7f272d35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180632 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180633 #edb/ref 1061 77834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e0d6400c-fcd8-5d0d-858f-8d1cf92c2a3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180634 #edb/ref 1061 77837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1dad9187-c0b1-5618-8f7a-6ee3c7b0a4c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180635 #edb/ref 1061 77838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f5e7a7a-0b7b-58c4-970d-eb187e7d2e39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1064 "In algebra, there are many different ways to represent multiplication. The following expressions all mean \"two times $y$\":\n$2y,\\qquad 2 \\cdot y,\\qquad 2 \\times y$.\n\nWhen a number is next to a variable, it means that we multiply them together. We can also represent multiplication using the dot symbol ($\\cdot$) or the familiar \"times\" symbol ($\\times$), though we tend to avoid using the ($\\times$) symbol because it looks a lot like the variable $x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1065 8167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dfb2834b-8f6f-55c4-b933-6f80cbf9e842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient** of two numbers means that we need to divide the first number by the second number.\n\nFor example, \"the quotient of $x$ and two\" means the same thing as \"$x$ divided by two\" and can be written as either expression below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2},\\qquad x \\div 2 \\,\n$$\n\nIn algebra, we prefer to represent division using the fraction bar instead of the ($\\div$) symbol." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1065 8168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "966707d2-a5e7-5ce1-a0b4-1c2991c80e3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1067 "The Division Sign in Algebra" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184145 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184145 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184145 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186183951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184145 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184146 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184146 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17